==Controversy==
On February 10<sup>th</sup> 2025, the [https://esportsworldcup.com/en Esports World Cup Foundation] announced VALORANT's inclusion in the 2025 iteration of its [[:esports:Esports_World_Cup/2025|Esports World Cup]]. <ref name="EWC Announcement">{{Cite web|url=https://valo2asia.com/ewc-valorant-2025-first-details-announced/|title=EWC VALORANT 2025 first details announced|author=Juandi|publisher=[[VALO2ASIA]]|date=2025-03-17}}</ref> This announcement was met with criticism by sections of the esports community, due to the Event being funded by the Saudi Arabian government's Public Investment Fund and controversies surrounding the Saudi government's human rights record. <ref name="ESI article">{{Cite web|url=https://esportsinsider.com/2025/02/esports-world-cup-foundation-riot-games-valorant-league-of-legends-tft|title=Riot Games partners with Esports World Cup Foundation — VALORANT, League of Legends and TFT added to EWC|author=Davide Xu|publisher=Esports Insider|date=2025-02-10}}</ref> Concerns were raised regarding the safety of women and LGBTQ+ individuals in the country, these concerns were exacerbated by a report that internal EWC documentation confirmed a ''VALORANT'' [[Game Changers]] event would take place, however this report was denied by [[Riot Games]] staff. <ref name="GC EWC event">{{cite web|url=https://www.readergrev.com/p/valorant-game-changers-esports-world-cup-saudi-arabia|title=Internal Esports World Cup document includes Valorant in 2025 event lineup|author=Mikhail Klimentov|date=2025-12-27}}</ref> 

Notable figures in the ''VALORANT'' community have spoken out in relation to the event, [[VCT]] broadcast talent, Josh "[[Sideshow]]" Wilkinson, has been one of the most vocal critics, calling for game developers, players and esports organisations to boycott the event. <ref name="Sideshow saudi">{{cite web|url=https://youtu.be/GIilD9qAzeA|title=Saudi Arabia is poisoning esports: We SHOULD Care.|author=[[Sideshow]]|date=2024-01-04}}</ref><ref name="Sideshow EWC">{{cite web|url=https://youtu.be/fBZjFYU-OX8|title=They own 40% of esports. Let's keep VALORANT.|author=[[Sideshow]]|date=2025-01-04}}</ref> Analyst for [[Gentle Mates]], Josh "[[jtay]]" Taylor has spoken out against the ''VALORANT'' '''Esports World Cup''', stating steps had been taken internally to make their position clear. <ref name="jtay ewc">{{cite web|url=https://x.com/JTayy_/status/1902177255926710658|title=I've also made steps privately with my organisation to make my position clear on it, and will not be attending unless absolutely necessary|author=[[jtay]]|date=2025-03-19}}</ref> Notable co-streamers: ex-[[Sentinels]] player Tyson "[[TenZ]]" Ngo and [[Sentinels]] co-streamer Tarik "[[tarik]]" Celik both publicly announced their criticisms of the event; TenZ stating his refusal to watch-party the tournament, and Tarik reportedly turning down "seven figures" {{Abbr|$USD|United States Dollars}} offers to host the event. <ref name="TenZ ewc">{{cite web|url=https://x.com/nanobombs/status/1922824750721597566|title=tenz will not watch/participate in the ewc|author=naya|date=2025-03-15}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.indiatodaygaming.com/news/story/tarik-turns-down-a-million-dollars-the-esports-world-cup-controversy-7764|title=Tarik Turns Down a Million Dollars – The Esports World Cup Controversy|publisher=India Today Gaming|date=2025-03-21}}</ref> The ''r/ValorantCompetitive'' esports community, with over 300,000 members as of May 2025, on [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reddit Reddit] announced that the community would be removing any posts related to the '''Esports World Cup''', citing sportswashing and human rights concerns related to the event. <ref name="ReaderGrev Vanity">{{Cite web|url=https://www.readergrev.com/p/exit-interview-anthony-vanity-malaspina-cloud9|title=Post scriptum: Valorant subreddit limits threads on Saudi-run Esports World Cup|author=[[Mikhail Klimentov]]|date=2025-03-14}}</ref>

